#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
ARACHNID - BRUTAL C TEST
========================
    python qa_c_brutal.py

A small inventory program in C (4 source files, 2 headers, a Makefile) with 21 memory bugs hidden the way they hide in
real code: a leak seen only through a second pointer, a `continue` that skips a free, a free repeated after a `break`
leaves the loop, a struct member freed twice, `sizeof nd` without brackets, a self-realloc behind a cast, ... and its
CLEAN TWIN: the same program with every bug fixed plus correct code that LOOKS like the bugs (realloc into a temporary,
`goto done` clean-up, free in both switch cases, free then reassign in a loop, sizeof(char *) for an array of pointers).

Both versions compile with gcc -Wall -Wextra; the clean twin also runs clean under AddressSanitizer.
Passes only if a full scan finds EVERY hidden bug on its exact line, and finds NOTHING AT ALL on the clean twin.
"""
